Ledger sources: the linked per-run prediction files above, [saved fold metrics](reports/fold_metrics.csv), [classifier/features/seed config](configs/protocol.yaml). This exhaustive list covers individual-fold crossings separately from the pooled-repeat and aggregate crossings; overlapping levels are not added together as independent evidence.

### 7.5 Interpreting “why”: observations, hypotheses and alternatives

**A. Directly supported observations**

The only above-benchmark averages come from strict GSE46306 within-study random CV. The real-only baseline already exceeds the benchmark; augmentation is not necessary for that result. Random oversampling and SMOTE also exceed 80.00% on average but are worse than that same baseline. Diffusion's three pooled crossings are confined to repeat 2. Their matched real-only repeat has 86.18% BA, so two diffusion seed crossings are worse than baseline; the other is only a small positive change. The consistency table includes every seed and fold, rather than highlighting that one favorable comparison.

Class balancing changes observed sensitivity and specificity. For strict GSE46306, diffusion's average sensitivity is higher than real-only but its specificity is lower; the loss in specificity outweighs the sensitivity increase in BA. The metric tables quantify that tradeoff. No dedicated experiment isolates whether it comes from class priors, synthetic feature geometry or another aspect of training. The only positive augmentation protocol-average BA changes relative to real-only are SMOTE under GSE99511 LOCO and random oversampling/SMOTE in reverse transfer; none reaches 80.00% in those protocols, and their consistency counts limit strong conclusions.

Training loss declines and numerical checks show finite synthetic beta values without observed exact/near-real duplicates at the predefined tolerance. Those observations do not explain a classification benefit, because diffusion's aggregate matched BA changes are negative throughout.

**B. Plausible mechanisms that remain unproven**

Balancing changes LDA's empirical priors from observed real class frequencies to equal priors, and synthetic/repeated rows also change its estimated means/covariance. Either could shift the sensitivity/specificity tradeoff or decision boundary. Useful joint-pattern learning by diffusion is a hypothesis, not an established finding here. To attribute benefits to better joint modeling, a dedicated comparison separating class-prior effects and sample-distribution effects would be needed.

Feature reduction to 50 sites and covariance shrinkage may stabilize a small-sample classifier, but all four branches use them, so this experiment cannot attribute method differences to those shared choices. Within-study random splits may share technical or cohort patterns that are absent in cross-study transfer. The weaker transfer results are compatible with distribution shift but do not establish its cause.

The fixed 100-step linear DDPM schedule also leaves substantial terminal signal: product(1−beta_t) is about 0.3636. Training's final noised distribution therefore need not match the standard Gaussian used to begin reverse sampling. This is a potential engineering limitation, not a tested explanation of the negative results; no schedule or duration was changed after inspecting performance.

**C. Alternative explanations and limitations**

Small folds, split/seed variation, inherited preprocessing differences, cohort/HPV composition and chip-related patterns could contribute. No analysis identifies which of these causes the observed scores. All six verified GSE99511 chips contain both classes, but their class proportions and held-out results vary. GSE46306 Normal/CIN3 status also tracks HPV status in its metadata, limiting interpretation as HPV-positive clinical triage. Absent person identifiers prevent excluding repeated-person overlap with certainty.

Both datasets had been examined previously; repeated development comparisons make these exploratory findings, even with the newly fixed protocol. The current training-only code and artifact checks provide no direct evidence of held-out fitting or inconsistent metric calculation. They cannot certify every earlier normalization/masking step or prove absence of every form of leakage. No affected primary result was discovered that must be relabeled as a leakage-driven success, but unverified patient identity and deposited preprocessing remain limitations. There is no causal evidence that diffusion learned disease biology, corrected batch effects or improved a clinical decision process.

High ordinary accuracy or AUC without BA ≥80.00% is explicitly insufficient. For example, strict-cohort diffusion has mean ROC AUC 0.8551 but mean BA 77.60%; it does not meet the benchmark at the fixed operating threshold. Reconciled random oversampling/SMOTE also have AUC above 0.8000 while their BA means stay below 80.00%. No model or threshold was changed to turn these into favorable results.

## 8. Generator diagnostics: what was observed and what it does not prove

Each fit saved per-class real/generated feature means and standard deviations; real/generated correlation matrices and differences; same-class nearest-training distances; real-to-real distances excluding self; duplicate checks; boundary/invalid counts; training losses; runtime; and parameter count. An earlier checkpoint-replay verifier also saved nearest distances to **all** real training rows, not just the same class. No held-out sample was used to filter or select generations.

| Diagnostic | Observed value |
| --- | --- |
| Separate generator fits | 162 |
| Class-conditioned diagnostic blocks | 324 |
| Invalid generated values | 0 |
| Exact generated-to-real duplicate rows (same class) | 0 |
| Near generated-to-real rows, max-coordinate tolerance 1e-6 (same class) | 0 |
| Exact/near generated-to-real duplicate rows across all classes | 0 / 0 |
| Generated-internal exact/near duplicate pairs | 0 / 0 |
| Maximum boundary fraction (beta ≤0.0001 or ≥0.9999) | 0.00% |
| Median mean-absolute correlation difference | 0.2033 |
| Median first/last logged training MSE | 1.0067 / 0.3074 |

Sources: [full per-fit diagnostics](outputs/diagnostics/primary/), [diagnostic summary](reports/diagnostic_summary.csv), [all-training neighbor diagnostics](reports/all_training_neighbor_diagnostics.csv), [artifact verification](reports/artifact_verification.json), [generator config](configs/diffusion.yaml). Correlation differences compare feature pairs in selected beta space. Zero boundary/duplicate flags mean only that these defined checks did not detect those patterns; they do not rule out subtler memorization, partial collapse, lack of biological realism or privacy risk. Low noise-prediction MSE is a training-objective result, not evidence of useful augmentation.

Useful saved figures: [per-chip BA](reports/figures/per_chip.png), [training losses](reports/figures/training_losses.png), [correlation/boundary distributions](reports/figures/synthesis_quality.png), [same-class distance comparisons](reports/figures/synthesis_distances.png), [all-training distance comparisons](reports/figures/all_training_neighbor_distances.png). Confusion plots under [reports/figures](reports/figures/) use repeat 0 and predefined augmentation seed 17, not the best seed; they are illustrative single streams, not ensemble confusions or averages across people repeated in CV.

## 9. Interpretation across datasets and evidence-based conclusions

**Did diffusion help relative to real-only?** No protocol-average matched BA improved. Its mean BA changes are negative for both within-dataset cohorts, GSE99511 secondary CV and all transfer evaluations. Individual fold/seed wins do not overturn those averages.

**Did diffusion beat simpler augmentation?** Not consistently, and not in a way supporting a general benefit. The comparison below subtracts random-oversampling and SMOTE protocol means from diffusion's mean. Positive numbers mean diffusion is higher; the real-only differences remain negative.

| Protocol | Diffusion − real (pp) | Diffusion − random (pp) | Diffusion − SMOTE (pp) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GSE99511_loco | -2.18 | -2.05 | -5.29 |
| GSE99511_cv | -4.85 | -1.46 | +0.55 |
| GSE46306_strict_cv | -4.80 | -2.53 | -3.64 |
| GSE46306_reconciled_cv | -9.60 | -6.64 | -6.51 |
| transfer_99511_strict | -9.56 | +3.24 | -2.40 |
| transfer_99511_reconciled | -11.57 | +2.04 | -3.06 |
| transfer_46306_99511 | -1.65 | -3.04 | -2.05 |

Sources: [recomputed prediction-based protocol means](outputs/predictions/primary/), [existing summary](reports/summary.csv), [fixed classifier/features config](configs/protocol.yaml). Diffusion exceeds SMOTE slightly in GSE99511 secondary random CV and exceeds random oversampling in the two GSE99511-source transfer target analyses, while still remaining below real-only in each. It is not a consistent winner over simpler augmentation.

**Were gains consistent across datasets?** No. GSE99511's modest SMOTE LOCO gain does not reproduce as a positive average within GSE46306; random CV and LOCO themselves are different tests. Diffusion shows no positive protocol-average matched BA on either study.

**Did within-study performance transfer across studies?** The evidence does not support reliable cross-study generalization for this fixed pipeline. Strict-GSE46306 within-study real-only BA exceeds 80.00%, but transfer scores in both directions are much lower and all transfer means stay below 80.00%. This demonstrates limited observed transfer performance, not proof that methylation has no useful biological signal or that any particular batch mechanism caused failure. Different target cohorts, real class counts, preprocessing and train/test definitions prevent ranking all protocol scores as if they were the same exam.

**Was the benchmark observed?** Yes: strict GSE46306 repeated stratified CV protocol means cross it for real-only (82.40%), random oversampling (80.13%) and SMOTE (81.24%). Diffusion's strict mean is 77.60%. Its three individual repeat-2 seed streams cross it, including one only slightly above the same-repeat real-only baseline; none is promoted to the primary headline. Reconciled real-only crosses it in one repeat but not its protocol mean. The exhaustive fold ledger records additional local crossings, not additional successful aggregate protocols. No LOCO or transfer protocol mean crosses it.

**What remains uncertain?** Whether other independently prespecified generators, source-only tuning or substantially larger cohorts could help; how much observed variation reflects biology, cohort sampling, HPV status, technical processing or chip effects; whether unrecorded person relationships exist; and whether performance would hold in a genuinely independent clinical cohort. These questions were not answered by this implementation.

### Principal limitations

The real cohorts are small (64, 37 and 38 sample IDs), while the available inputs include hundreds of thousands of CpGs. Fold-local feature selection reduces dimensionality for modeling but can still be unstable. Selected feature associations and synthesis diagnostics do not establish biological mechanisms. Synthetic samples add rows, not independent people or new clinical evidence.

GSE99511 chip composition and held-out results vary; GSE46306 lacks verified grouping and has a truncated local series file, a metadata-missing diagnosis reconciled only in sensitivity, contradictory scale descriptions and incompletely established deposited filtering provenance. Unique sample IDs do not establish unique people. GSE99511 self-samples and GSE46306 cervical tissue differ in collection context, and source preprocessing differs. Both studies were previously examined, so even cross-study results are exploratory. No untouched clinical validation, clinical utility assessment or significance claim is provided.

## 10. Inconsistencies and missing evidence found during this summary

1. **No primary prediction/metric inconsistency found.** All 540 primary records match the current source/config/data-manifest/split fingerprint, and recalculated fold/pooled/protocol scores agree with existing metric CSV/JSON values within floating-point tolerance. Saved labels agree with cohort manifests, threshold decisions agree with probabilities, and split IDs are disjoint. Precision and F1 were missing from the earlier reports, but were calculable from predictions and are included here without altering those reports.
2. **A damaged input is already documented.** The local GSE46306 series gzip is truncated. Readable metadata and representative rows were usable; the complete processed supplement supplies model inputs. This is not silently treated as a fully verified series table. Original exclusion of cg00979000 remains unexplained.
3. **Person/batch and historical preprocessing evidence is incomplete.** There are no verified GSE46306 chip or person groups, no complete person-identity guarantee for either dataset, and no complete historical IDAT processing execution record. Source filenames and intended pipeline code do not prove every historical mask flag. These gaps limit stronger claims, despite the fold-local current implementation.
4. **Smoke results have a different code/config fingerprint.** Twelve non-scientific smoke runs were saved, but their fingerprint differs from the final primary fingerprint. They demonstrate an earlier smoke snapshot, not a verified exact-final-code smoke rerun. They are excluded from all scientific tables. This summary did not rerun them because training is prohibited for this task.
5. **Some YAML-looking settings are fixed in code.** The current numeric values agree, but the augmentation function hardcodes the multiplier 2, the metric function hardcodes threshold 0.5/benchmark 0.8, and duplicate diagnostics hardcode their tolerances. The DDPM implementation always uses a linear schedule despite the descriptive schedule field. Editing those YAML labels alone would not fully reconfigure the implementation. This does not change the executed fixed-primary results; it is a reproducibility/configurability caveat, not a silent fix.
6. **Resume is completed-run reuse, not optimizer continuation.** The runner skips completed compatible prediction/metric pairs. Failed/incomplete fits restart. Although generator checkpoints include optimizer state, no mid-training restoration path is implemented. Classifier weights/full fitted LDA objects and raw generated matrices are not saved; prediction files support metric recalculation, and generator checkpoint sidecars support audit/replay. Reproducing a fitted classifier itself requires rerunning its training pipeline.
7. **Fingerprint scope has limits.** It covers Python files under `src`, YAML configs, source-hash manifest and split manifest. It does not include wrapper scripts, tests, dependency versions or the external data bytes directly. The runner separately verifies actual external source hashes, and versions are recorded, but automatic reuse does not independently invalidate a changed dependency environment or wrapper script. Saved verification JSONs are evidence of earlier checks, not immutable certificates.
8. **Timing and diagnostic scope are bounded.** The 382-second timer is the primary runner's internal interval, not setup-to-finish elapsed time. Per-method timings omit shared preprocessing. Initial diagnostics use same-class neighbor references; an earlier sampler-replay verification supplements them with all-class training references. No new diagnoses, raw-data repairs, training runs, tuning or pipeline changes were performed for this summary.

Sources: [metric code](src/methylation_diffusion/evaluation.py), [augmentation code](src/methylation_diffusion/augmentation.py), [DDPM](src/methylation_diffusion/diffusion.py), [resume/fingerprint code](src/methylation_diffusion/common.py), [runner](src/methylation_diffusion/runner.py), [smoke environment](outputs/logs/smoke_environment.json), [primary environment](outputs/logs/primary_environment.json), [resume verification](reports/resume_verification.json), [artifact verification](reports/artifact_verification.json).

## 11. Reproducibility and file map

### Configuration and dependency records

- [configs/protocol.yaml](configs/protocol.yaml): 50 CpGs, filter thresholds, fixed LDA parameters/threshold, augmentation and CV seeds, benchmark and no tuning.
- [configs/diffusion.yaml](configs/diffusion.yaml): architecture, transforms, noise schedule endpoints/steps, optimizer budget and CPU settings.
- [configs/experiments.yaml](configs/experiments.yaml): four methods and seven declared protocol names; no optional feature-count sensitivity enabled. The split manifest determines the actual run partitions.
- [configs/paths.example.yaml](configs/paths.example.yaml): default sibling external data path. Optional `configs/paths.local.yaml` overrides it and is Git-ignored; it was not needed for this default-path execution. Do not put datasets inside the project.
- [requirements.lock.txt](requirements.lock.txt), [requirements.txt](requirements.txt), [pyproject.toml](pyproject.toml), [executed environment](outputs/logs/primary_environment.json): Python 3.10.16; NumPy 2.2.6; pandas 2.3.3; SciPy 1.15.3; scikit-learn 1.7.2; PyArrow 25.0.1; PyTorch 2.14.1 CPU. The source supports Python ≥3.10, but these are the recorded versions for the actual run.

The executed `.venv/pyvenv.cfg` records `include-system-site-packages = true`. The fresh-install commands below use an isolated environment and the saved dependency lock rather than requiring that inherited-package setting.

The following commands describe the implemented pipeline and its reproducible entry points. **They are reference instructions, not commands rerun while writing this summary.** `pytest` and the smoke command include tiny training; the full experiment command trains missing/incompatible runs. Do not run those when a documentation-only recalculation is intended.

```powershell
Set-Location 'D:\coding\python\ML RESEARCH PROJECT\diffusion+methylation'

# Fresh installation, if needed; the executed project already has .venv.
py -3.10 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.lock.txt
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -e . --no-deps

# Optional path override; edit data_root using forward slashes in YAML.
Copy-Item configs\paths.example.yaml configs\paths.local.yaml

# Audit, split creation, checks, non-scientific smoke, and primary suite.
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\audit_data.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\supplement_audit.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\make_splits.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\run_smoke_test.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\run_experiments.py

# Verify saved fitted artifacts and regenerate existing aggregate reports.
# verify_results replays saved generator sampling without retraining.
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\verify_results.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\build_report.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\verify_html.py
Start-Process reports\results.html
```

`build_report.py` recalculates metrics from predictions without training, but it **rewrites README.md and the existing reports**, so it was deliberately not used for this documentation-only task. `verify_results.py` also rewrites diagnostic/verification artifacts; its earlier saved verification was read instead. `verify_html.py` uses installed Chrome/Edge headless rendering and verifies report-table/CSV agreement. All three are documented entry points, not harmless read-only commands in every respect.

Run/resume the saved primary suite with `.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\run_experiments.py`. Completed compatible runs are skipped; the saved resume verification checked 162 unchanged checkpoint files and zero newly completed runs on a one-partition resume. A specific protocol can be selected with `--experiment GSE99511_loco` or any of the seven names in the results tables. `--limit 1` limits execution to one partition and must not be called a full study.

